    "finish_elapsed_s": 10.617282063991297,
    "text": "A refrigerator works by moving heat from inside to outside, like carrying warmth away in a bucket. A special liquid called refrigerant flows through pipes. First, it evaporates inside the fridge, soaking up heat and turning into a gas. That gas gets squeezed by a compressor, which makes it hot. Then it flows through coils behind or under the fridge, where the heat escapes into your kitchen. As it cools, the refrigerant turns back into a liquid and cycles around again. Because heat keeps getting removed, the inside stays cold, keeping your food fresh. The fridge doesn't create cold—it just moves heat out!"
  }
